or vice versa, you know,

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:25.431
the history of Donald Trump
written by Hillary Clinton.

00:09:25.431 --> 00:09:28.334 align:start size:84% position:16%
Regardless of the
facts on the ground,

00:09:28.334 --> 00:09:30.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we can talk about
all of the complex facets

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:34.140 align:start size:88% position:13%
of Columbus' life, it's
simply preposterous

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:36.676
to take his chief
political rival

00:09:36.676 --> 00:09:38.678
as the gospel truth on the man,

00:09:38.678 --> 00:09:41.814 align:start size:97% position:3%
especially when that
narrative is so contradicted

00:09:41.814 --> 00:09:43.749 align:start size:84% position:16%
by many other
esteemed historians

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:45.885
and people who were
there at the time.

00:09:45.885 --> 00:09:47.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think of Bartolome
de Las Casas,

00:09:47.853 --> 00:09:50.189 align:start size:88% position:13%
the first resident
bishop of the Americas.

00:09:50.189 --> 00:09:52.992 align:start size:97% position:3%
Bartolome de Las Casas was
honest about Columbus' faults,

00:09:52.992 --> 00:09:55.494 align:start size:88% position:13%
and he said the man
didn't govern perfectly,

00:09:55.494 --> 00:09:57.563
but he's considered one
of the greatest defenders

00:09:57.563 --> 00:09:58.965
of the Native Americans,

00:09:58.965 --> 00:10:02.034 align:start size:97% position:3%
and yet de Las Casas
remained a lifelong admirer

00:10:02.034 --> 00:10:03.302
of Christopher Columbus.

00:10:03.302 --> 00:10:04.737
And I think we need
to take into account

00:10:04.737 --> 00:10:06.238 align:start size:84% position:16%
that historical
perspective as well

00:10:06.238 --> 00:10:09.108
before we throw the guy
over the ship as it were.

00:10:09.108 --> 00:10:10.643 align:start size:81% position:19%
- All right, so
with that in mind,

00:10:10.643 --> 00:10:12.511
let's spend our remaining time
talking about the good stuff.

00:10:12.511 --> 00:10:13.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
We know the
controversies are coming.

00:10:13.946 --> 00:10:15.014
We know what they're gonna say.

00:10:15.014 --> 00:10:16.515
Okay, so be it.

00:10:16.515 --> 00:10:18.150
And by the way, none of these
people are leaving America.

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:19.619
None of these people
are doing anything

00:10:19.619 --> 00:10:21.153
other than complaining
and everything else.

00:10:21.153 --> 00:10:24.123
But let's be productive here
and get some real knowledge.

00:10:24.123 --> 00:10:27.693 align:start size:88% position:13%
Just on the exploration
part itself,

00:10:27.693 --> 00:10:28.928 align:start size:84% position:16%
are there any
interesting stories

00:10:28.928 --> 00:10:31.364
that sort of got him to decide,

00:10:31.364 --> 00:10:33.532
"Yeah, I'm gonna get in a
boat and see what happens?"

00:10:33.532 --> 00:10:35.167
It's a pretty crazy thing.

00:10:35.167 --> 00:10:38.170
I mean, the only comparable
thing we have in modern times

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:41.007 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that Elon Musk is trying
to send people to Mars

00:10:41.007 --> 00:10:42.642
or that kinda thing.

00:10:42.642 --> 00:10:45.144
- Yes, the thing
you need to know

00:10:45.144 --> 00:10:47.246 align:start size:84% position:16%
about Christopher
Columbus' exploration

00:10:47.246 --> 00:10:50.016
is that the man was unique.

00:10:50.016 --> 00:10:51.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is not something
to be looked at

00:10:51.851 --> 00:10:53.686
as the mere tides of history

00:10:53.686 --> 00:10:55.955
and someone was going to
discover the Americas,

00:10:55.955 --> 00:10:57.556
and, oh, you know,
if it wasn't Chris,

00:10:57.556 --> 00:10:59.058 align:start size:81% position:19%
it would have been
somebody else.

00:10:59.058 --> 00:11:03.229 align:start size:97% position:3%
No, Christopher Columbus
was so ruthlessly determined

00:11:03.229 --> 00:11:05.865
and ambitious to
find the sea route

00:11:05.865 --> 00:11:08.000 align:start size:84% position:16%
that he, first of
all, educated himself.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.404
He educated himself, became
literate, became a Spaniard.

00:11:11.404 --> 00:11:13.139
He was an Italian guy,
but he became a Spaniard,

00:11:13.139 --> 00:11:14.407 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause he thought
that was going to be

00:11:14.407 --> 00:11:16.876
basically his best
chance to go on over.

00:11:16.876 --> 00:11:18.244
He lobbied the king of Portugal.

00:11:18.244 --> 00:11:19.779
The king of Portugal refused.

00:11:19.779 --> 00:11:23.249 align:start size:97% position:3%
He then goes and lobbies
the king and queen of Spain.

00:11:23.249 --> 00:11:24.717
They refused as well,

00:11:24.717 --> 00:11:27.753 align:start position:0%
but through basically sheer
tyranny of will and personality,

00:11:27.753 --> 00:11:30.122 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
he finally gets
Isabella, Queen Isabella,

00:11:30.122 --> 00:11:32.825 line:0%
to relent and fund this voyage.

00:11:32.825 --> 00:11:35.828 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
He had already had a lot of
experience at sea before that.

00:11:35.828 --> 00:11:37.630
And then he sets to sea.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:38.864
What does he have,

00:11:38.864 --> 00:11:40.099 align:start size:84% position:16%
all these wonderful
tools at his disposal?

00:11:40.099 --> 00:11:42.902 align:start size:84% position:16%
No, he's got a
compass and the stars.

00:11:42.902 --> 00:11:45.171 align:start size:84% position:16%
He used dead reckoning
to make it across.

00:11:45.171 --> 00:11:46.772 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, you'll often hear
people today say,

00:11:46.772 --> 00:11:48.507
"Christopher Columbus,
he didn't even end up

00:11:48.507 --> 00:11:49.809
where he wanted to end up.

00:11:49.809 --> 00:11:51.343
He wanted to end up
in the East Indies.

00:11:51.343 --> 00:11:54.313 align:start size:97% position:3%
And what, he found Bermuda,"
or whatever, not Bermuda.

00:11:54.313 --> 00:11:56.549
He found The Bahamas,
he found the Caribbean.

00:11:56.549 --> 00:11:57.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you think,
well, I don't know,

00:11:57.850 --> 00:11:59.852
the guy had a compass
and he had the stars.

00:11:59.852 --> 00:12:00.753
How far would you make it?

00:12:00.753 --> 00:12:02.054
Probably not very far.

00:12:02.054 --> 00:12:03.489 align:start size:81% position:19%
He also had to
put down a mutiny.

00:12:03.489 --> 00:12:06.292 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I can't get to the 405
without my GPS, so I got you.

00:12:06.292 --> 00:12:08.294
- I know, of course
not, none of us can.

00:12:09.762 --> 00:12:13.032
On this voyage, as they were
going a far greater distance

00:12:13.032 --> 00:12:15.768
I think than many people
were prepared to undertake,

00:12:16.936 --> 00:12:18.471 align:start size:88% position:13%
Christopher Columbus had
to put down a mutiny.

00:12:18.471 --> 00:12:21.307
So, here you do see a little
bit of his leadership skill.

00:12:21.307 --> 00:12:23.509 align:start size:97% position:3%
Maybe he wasn't the greatest
governor of the Indies,

00:12:23.509 --> 00:12:25.277 align:start size:81% position:19%
but he did keep
these men in line,

00:12:25.277 --> 00:12:27.646
and they finally do spot land.

00:12:27.646 --> 00:12:31.851
And he I think owes a lot
of it to his faith in God.

00:12:33.018 --> 00:12:34.653
When he finally does
land in the Caribbean,

00:12:34.653 --> 00:12:36.422
the first island he lands at,

00:12:36.422 --> 00:12:39.725 align:start size:94% position:6%
he calls it San Salvador,
right, the Savior.

00:12:42.328 --> 00:12:45.231
He deals with all of these
difficult political problems.

00:12:45.231 --> 00:12:48.434
He is brought back
to Spain in chains,

00:12:48.434 --> 00:12:50.736
so he undergoes a
lot of persecution.

00:12:50.736 --> 00:12:53.706 align:start position:0%
And Columbus doesn't just
complain about this, by the way.

00:12:53.706 --> 00:12:55.541 align:start size:84% position:16%
He thinks he was
unjustly persecuted.

00:12:55.541 --> 00:12:57.710
I think he was largely
unjustly persecuted.

00:12:57.710 --> 00:12:59.345 align:start size:88% position:13%
There were some
cynical motives for it,

00:12:59.345 --> 00:13:01.680
which is that as long
as Christopher Columbus

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:03.182
was a criminal,

00:13:03.182 --> 00:13:04.884
then the crown didn't need to
pay him what they owed him.

00:13:04.884 --> 00:13:06.485
So, there were a lot of
political machinations

00:13:06.485 --> 00:13:07.720
going on in Spain.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:11.023
But he understood that
perhaps he was suffering

00:13:11.023 --> 00:13:12.258
because of his own failings,

00:13:12.258 --> 00:13:14.093
And he writes about
this in his journals.

00:13:14.093 --> 00:13:16.061
So, he actually does
look in on himself.

00:13:16.061 --> 00:13:19.765
And the two-dimensional
picture is just so silly,

00:13:19.765 --> 00:13:21.700
not to harp too much on
the criticisms of him,

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.502
but I think there
is an important one

00:13:23.502 --> 00:13:26.705
that we need to focus on
here, which is ourselves.

00:13:26.705 --> 00:13:29.008 align:start position:0%
In the spirit of Columbus
looking in on his own failings,

00:13:29.008 --> 00:13:30.476
we need to look in on ourselves.

00:13:30.476 --> 00:13:33.412 align:start size:97% position:3%
Without Christopher Columbus,
we would not be here.

00:13:33.412 --> 00:13:34.980
Without Christopher Columbus,

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:37.516
the Hispanic people
would not exist.

00:13:37.516 --> 00:13:40.386
There would be no such thing
as Latinos or Hispanics, right,

00:13:40.386 --> 00:13:42.888
which is Latino people
come from the mixture

00:13:42.888 --> 00:13:44.757 align:start size:81% position:19%
of the American
indigenous peoples

00:13:44.757 --> 00:13:46.058
and the Spaniards who come over.

00:13:46.058 --> 00:13:48.627
So, if you are an
Hispanic person,

00:13:48.627 --> 00:13:52.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
you owe your very
ethnic identity to
Christopher Columbus.

00:13:52.131 --> 00:13:55.034 align:start position:0%
And we, of course, owe our
existence here in the New World.

00:13:55.034 --> 00:13:57.369 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so I think before
we cast stones,

00:13:57.369 --> 00:13:59.104
we should recognize
that we are standing

00:13:59.104 --> 00:14:01.507
on the shoulders of giants,
this giant in particular,

00:14:01.507 --> 00:14:03.275
and yet we think
that we're flying.

00:14:04.677 --> 00:14:06.679
- What else don't we know
about those first days

00:14:06.679 --> 00:14:08.614
when he reaches the New World?

00:14:08.614 --> 00:14:10.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is there anything
particularly interesting?

00:14:11.684 --> 00:14:13.219
It's just like hard to...

00:14:13.219 --> 00:14:14.920
Well, how long did it take
by the way to get across?

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:16.956 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I forget the exact
number of days.

00:14:16.956 --> 00:14:19.291
I used to know this pretty well,

00:14:19.291 --> 00:14:21.093
but I forget the exact timing.

00:14:21.093 --> 00:14:22.428 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are some
interesting stories though

00:14:22.428 --> 00:14:24.763
when he lands the first time,

00:14:24.763 --> 00:14:26.732
and I think this is
also really important

00:14:26.732 --> 00:14:29.268 align:start size:88% position:13%
when we're talking about
the Native Americans,

00:14:29.268 --> 00:14:30.870 align:start size:84% position:16%
the indigenous peoples
from the region,

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:32.638
'cause I don't mean to downplay

00:14:32.638 --> 00:14:34.206
the suffering that they endured.

00:14:34.206 --> 00:14:36.542 align:start size:88% position:13%
Many of them did endure
a lot of suffering.

00:14:36.542 --> 00:14:40.045 align:start size:88% position:13%
But the Native Americans
that he encountered,

00:14:40.045 --> 00:14:42.214
I mentioned this a
little bit earlier,

00:14:42.214 --> 00:14:43.582
were all sorts of
different people,

00:14:43.582 --> 00:14:44.817
and some of them were good

00:14:44.817 --> 00:14:46.318
and some of them
were not so good.

00:14:46.318 --> 00:14:49.622
And some of the groups of
native Caribbeans were good

00:14:49.622 --> 00:14:50.723
and some of them
were not so good.

00:14:50.723 --> 00:14:52.625
And the thing we find about them

00:14:52.625 --> 00:14:55.027
when we read Columbus' writings,

00:14:55.027 --> 00:14:56.228 align:start size:88% position:13%
when we read the
writings of other people

00:14:56.228 --> 00:14:57.696
who were on the voyage,

00:14:57.696 --> 00:15:01.367
is that those indigenous
peoples were a lot like us.

00:15:01.367 --> 00:15:03.068
That is, they were people

00:15:03.068 --> 00:15:04.370 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they had some
good characteristics

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:05.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they had some
bad characteristics,

00:15:05.804 --> 00:15:09.074 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the way that these
peoples developed over time.

00:15:09.074 --> 00:15:11.110 align:start size:84% position:16%
When you're talking
about that interaction

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:12.678
of Columbus with
the natives there,

00:15:12.678 --> 00:15:17.683
you're talking about the first
modern colonial interactions

00:15:19.451 --> 00:15:20.686
in the modern era.

00:15:20.686 --> 00:15:22.821
And so if there were
going to be problems,

00:15:22.821 --> 00:15:24.089 align:start size:84% position:16%
if there were going
to be failures,

00:15:24.089 --> 00:15:25.925
if there were going
to be mistakes made,

00:15:25.925 --> 00:15:27.793 align:start size:88% position:13%
recognize you're
talking about something

00:15:27.793 --> 00:15:31.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
that is so historic,
that Columbus is
credited in many ways

00:15:31.664 --> 00:15:34.934
with giving birth
to this modern era

00:15:34.934 --> 00:15:36.702
that we're all living in.

00:15:36.702 --> 00:15:39.772
- Would you like
to see the modern

00:15:39.772 --> 00:15:42.908 align:start size:88% position:13%
American-Italian
community stand up more

00:15:42.908 --> 00:15:44.810
for Columbus' legacy?

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:46.378
I mean, that episode
of "The Sopranos,"

00:15:46.378 --> 00:15:47.646 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know the one
you're talking about,

00:15:47.646 --> 00:15:49.415
it's the very famous
Columbus Day episode

00:15:49.415 --> 00:15:50.883 align:start size:84% position:16%
when they're trying
to tear down,

00:15:50.883 --> 00:15:52.584 align:start size:94% position:6%
some activists are trying
to tear down a statue

00:15:52.584 --> 00:15:53.585
somewhere in Jersey.

00:15:54.753 --> 00:15:56.388 align:start size:84% position:16%
I wonder, should the
Italian community

00:15:56.388 --> 00:15:59.024
be getting more up
in arms over this?

00:16:00.392 --> 00:16:02.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I do think that the Italians
have a special role here

00:16:02.594 --> 00:16:03.963
in defending Columbus.

00:16:03.963 --> 00:16:05.597
I don't love identity politics,

00:16:05.597 --> 00:16:07.633
and it's not like I wake up
every morning and I think,

00:16:07.633 --> 00:16:10.069
"Gosh, I am proudest of the fact

00:16:10.069 --> 00:16:13.272
that my skin is a little
swarthy and I eat cannoli,"

00:16:13.272 --> 00:16:14.707
and things like that.

00:16:14.707 --> 00:16:17.309
But the Italians have
a special role here.

00:16:17.309 --> 00:16:19.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
If they're not going to
defend Columbus, who will?

00:16:19.178 --> 00:16:22.181
And the Italians
ought to be happy

00:16:22.181 --> 00:16:23.949 align:start size:84% position:16%
about their
influence on America.

00:16:25.751 --> 00:16:27.419
First of all, America is
named after an Italian,

00:16:27.419 --> 00:16:29.388
Amerigo Vespucci, the mapmaker.

00:16:29.388 --> 00:16:30.923
You've got the Verrazano Bridge.

00:16:30.923 --> 00:16:33.559
You've got Christopher
Columbus, obviously,

00:16:33.559 --> 00:16:34.994
so many places named after him.

00:16:34.994 --> 00:16:37.262 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think this raises
another question.

00:16:37.262 --> 00:16:41.233 align:start size:97% position:3%
Why are towns, universities,
state, countries,

00:16:41.233 --> 00:16:43.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
whole countries, named
after Christopher Columbus?

00:16:43.969 --> 00:16:47.272
Why did people once admire him

00:16:47.272 --> 00:16:49.908 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they don't
admire him anymore?

00:16:49.908 --> 00:16:51.210
I suppose part of that might be

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.278
because unscrupulous journalists

00:16:53.278 --> 00:16:55.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
are relying on ridiculous
historical records,

00:16:57.116 --> 00:16:59.551
but I suspect it has to
do more with ourselves.

00:16:59.551 --> 00:17:04.356 align:start position:0%
We don't like ourselves so
much anymore as a civilization,

00:17:04.356 --> 00:17:06.859
and Columbus, because
he played such a role

00:17:06.859 --> 00:17:10.129 align:start size:97% position:3%
in developing that
civilization and embodying it,

00:17:10.129 --> 00:17:14.500
I think he bears a lot of the
brunt of this sort of thing.

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:16.101 align:start size:84% position:16%
You asked for
interesting episodes

00:17:16.101 --> 00:17:18.137
from those first
encounters he had,

00:17:18.137 --> 00:17:21.106 align:start size:97% position:3%
well, most of what we see
from those earliest encounters

00:17:21.106 --> 00:17:22.941
between Columbus and the natives

00:17:22.941 --> 00:17:25.711 align:start size:88% position:13%
are him imploring people
to treat them well,

00:17:27.146 --> 00:17:28.847 align:start size:88% position:13%
please, Spaniards, do
not deceive the natives,

00:17:28.847 --> 00:17:30.282
do not deceive them in trade,

00:17:30.282 --> 00:17:32.351
do not be too harsh on them.

00:17:32.351 --> 00:17:36.588 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that side of the man,
you never really get to see.

00:17:36.588 --> 00:17:40.793 align:start size:88% position:13%
Later on, more
difficult circumstances
developed, of course.

00:17:40.793 --> 00:17:42.461
No one is denying that.

00:17:42.461 --> 00:17:44.530
It reminds me of
Chesterton's fence.

00:17:46.231 --> 00:17:49.301
G.K. Chesterton, the English
writer, has this famous idea

00:17:49.301 --> 00:17:52.204 align:start size:88% position:13%
that there are two kinds
of reformers, right?

00:17:52.204 --> 00:17:54.339
The reformer who goes
up and sees a fence

00:17:54.339 --> 00:17:56.341 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the middle of nowhere,
just a white fence,

00:17:56.341 --> 00:17:57.609
it doesn't seem to
serve any purpose,

00:17:57.609 --> 00:17:59.411
and who says, "Tear
down that fence.

00:18:00.279 --> 00:18:01.780
I have no idea why that fence

00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:03.282
was erected in the first place."

00:18:03.282 --> 00:18:05.217
And then there's another
type of reformer who says,

00:18:05.217 --> 00:18:06.418
"Hold on a second,

00:18:08.253 --> 00:18:10.322 line:0%
if you do not know the
purpose of that fence,

00:18:10.322 --> 00:18:12.224 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
if you don't know why that was
erected in the first place,

00:18:12.224 --> 00:18:13.926 line:0%
then don't touch it.

00:18:13.926 --> 00:18:15.794 line:0%
Go figure out why it was
erected in the first place

00:18:15.794 --> 00:18:17.296 line:0%
and then tear it down.

00:18:17.296 --> 00:18:19.364 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that's what
we should begin with here,

00:18:19.364 --> 00:18:21.834
is a little bit of
humility to say,

00:18:21.834 --> 00:18:24.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Why was this man admired
for so very long?"

00:18:24.103 --> 00:18:28.874
Well, maybe shockingly,
it's us here in 2021

00:18:28.874 --> 00:18:30.342
who are missing something,

00:18:30.342 --> 00:18:32.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's not just the
diluted old, awful people

00:18:32.077 --> 00:18:35.180
who came before us that
we've gotta tear down.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:38.884 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Knowles, that kinda feels
like the right ending.

00:18:38.884 --> 00:18:40.719
(Michael laughing)

00:18:40.719 --> 00:18:43.322 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think you did it, you
brought the old into the new,

00:18:43.322 --> 00:18:46.258 align:start size:88% position:13%
you brought the Old
World to the New World.

00:18:48.393 --> 00:18:49.962
We bridged the sea.

00:18:49.962 --> 00:18:52.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
- (laughing) We've done
it, we've reached the sea.

00:18:52.498 --> 00:18:55.567
I hope we can defend the
great Christopher Columbus.

00:18:55.567 --> 00:18:57.803
I hope people don't
tear down his statues

00:18:57.803 --> 00:18:59.505
too much into the near future,

00:18:59.505 --> 00:19:00.739
because the thing is,

00:19:00.739 --> 00:19:03.542
when you tear down
your entire country,

00:19:03.542 --> 00:19:05.844
when you tear down your
entire civilization,

00:19:07.179 --> 00:19:09.381 align:start size:94% position:6%
one, some of those
statutes might clobber you

00:19:09.381 --> 00:19:10.749 align:start size:84% position:16%
and clobber the
things that you love,

00:19:10.749 --> 00:19:12.918
but you're going to end
up living amid ruins.

00:19:12.918 --> 00:19:15.954
And all cultures,
all civilizations,

00:19:15.954 --> 00:19:17.523 align:start size:84% position:16%
are gonna have to
admire certain people.

00:19:17.523 --> 00:19:19.625 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're gonna have to
admire certain values.

00:19:20.826 --> 00:19:22.060
I wanna live in a civilization

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:23.996
that admires the great
men who came before us,

00:19:23.996 --> 00:19:25.464
who gave us our culture,

00:19:25.464 --> 00:19:30.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
that admires the intrepid,
determined, faithful,

00:19:31.136 --> 00:19:35.174 align:start size:84% position:16%
intelligent, ambitious
sort of episodes

00:19:35.174 --> 00:19:37.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I think are
really well typified

00:19:37.476 --> 00:19:40.012
and really well exemplified
by Christopher Columbus,

00:19:40.012 --> 00:19:42.181
or we're gonna admire
a lot of the bad guys

00:19:42.181 --> 00:19:44.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
who are running around running
roughshod over our culture.

00:19:44.683 --> 00:19:45.918
Make your choice.

00:19:45.918 --> 00:19:47.619
Everybody's gotta
serve somebody.

00:19:47.619 --> 00:19:50.389 align:start size:97% position:3%
- In many ways, that's exactly
what the whole damn thing

00:19:50.389 --> 00:19:51.623
is about right now,

00:19:51.623 --> 00:19:53.358
and Knowles, you are
the living embodiment

00:19:53.358 --> 00:19:54.760
of Christopher Columbus

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:58.263 align:start size:97% position:3%
because you left Los
Angeles for the unchartered,

00:19:58.263 --> 00:20:02.834
the unknown county,
country of Tennessee.

00:20:02.834 --> 00:20:04.102 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's a country, right?
- This is true,

00:20:04.102 --> 00:20:05.971 align:start size:84% position:16%
